{
  "header": [
    "UNALLOCATED — §6.11(b): responses arriving out of order on one connection are routed to their",
    "callers by request_id.",
    "",
    "Batch 2. Floor by §9.0's prose, no §9.1 row (F14). This is the requester-side obligation the",
    "reference check `connectivity/request_id_echoed` CITES; the responder-side echo it MEASURES is",
    "ECP-R6 (F11)."
  ],
  "id_status": "unallocated",
  "under": "",
  "title": "When a counterparty answers a peer's outbound EXECUTEs out of order on one connection, each originating call receives its own response",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §6.11(b)",
  "spec_also": [
    "§3.2 (Correlation)",
    "§6.5 ('Root is EXECUTE_RESPONSE? -> Correlate by request_id, deliver to waiting caller')",
    "§6.11 'Profile applicability'",
    "§9.0"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "host-seam",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F14",
  "reading": [
    "§6.11(b): \"Response routing MUST tolerate out-of-order replies on a single connection. The",
    "`request_id` field on each `EXECUTE_RESPONSE` (§3 wire format) is the demultiplexing key; responses MUST be routed to the",
    "awaiting caller by `request_id`, irrespective of receive order on the wire.\"",
    "",
    "TWO MUSTS, ONE OBLIGATION: \"tolerate out-of-order\" is what \"route by request_id irrespective of order\"",
    "produces. A peer can fail it two ways — deliver response #2 to caller #1 (routing by arrival order),",
    "or discard/hang on an unexpected id — and both are the same failure of the one obligation.",
    "",
    "WHAT ONLY THE SEAM CAN SEE. The suite can SEND responses out of order; only the originating handler",
    "knows which response each call RECEIVED. So the probe handler reports, per call, the result it got,",
    "and the responses carry distinguishable results. This is the precondition that makes (b) measurable at",
    "all, and why it is host-seam.",
    "",
    "SINGLE CONNECTION IS PART OF THE INPUT. On a dial-per-dispatch peer, each response arrives alone on its",
    "own connection and \"out of order on a single connection\" is unconstructible -> SKIP naming the",
    "mechanism (from the (a) file's witness), not PASS."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [
      "as UNALLOCATED-reentry-no-serialization-across-await"
    ],
    "identities": [
      "caller",
      "suite-as-counterparty"
    ],
    "handlers": [
      "originating-probe (as above), reporting for each originated call the `result` it received"
    ],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "origination_trigger"
    ]
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "reversed-replies-routed",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "the probe handler originates #1 then #2 on one pooled connection; the suite answers #2 FIRST, then #1, each with a result naming its own request_id",
      "why": "§6.11(b).",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "listen_as_counterparty"
        },
        {
          "op": "trigger_origination",
          "count": 2
        },
        {
          "op": "receive_inbound_executes",
          "capture": "pair",
          "count": 2
        },
        {
          "op": "respond_in_order",
          "order": [
            "second",
            "first"
          ],
          "result": "a primitive/any entity whose data names the request_id it answers"
        },
        {
          "op": "read_probe_handler_report",
          "capture": "report"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "each_call_received_its_own_result",
          "capture": "report"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the same, answered IN order",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY. Proves the report is faithful and the result payloads distinguishable: in-order replies MUST route correctly on any working peer. If THIS arm fails, the harness or the seam is broken and the run is SKIP, not FAIL — the conformant arm's failure only means something when this one passes.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "respond_in_order",
          "order": [
            "first",
            "second"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "read_probe_handler_report",
          "capture": "report_in_order"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "each_call_received_its_own_result",
          "capture": "report_in_order"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a pooled client that pairs each write with the next frame read",
      "expect": "call #1 receives #2's result and vice versa",
      "why": "the arrival-order router; indistinguishable from a correct one whenever the counterparty happens to answer in order, which is why the reversal is the probe."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "",
    "oracle_note": "cited, not measured, by connectivity/request_id_echoed (F11)."
  }
}
